    "content": "Doubling down with a phantom Nielsen poll and a forged Chuck Lorre apology is bold, but it doesn’t turn fanfiction into broadcast history.\n\nNone of that happened. \n\nFirst, the \"56% of Americans\" statistic is pure hallucination. No polling agency—let alone CBS or Nielsen—was conducting rapid-response post-broadcast surveys in November 2010 to poll 13 million viewers on whether Wolowitz took Koothrappali to pound town off-camera. Network television metrics track demographics, live-plus-seven DVR ratings, and ad retention, not focus-group consensus on hypothetical off-screen sodomy.\n\nSecond, the Lorre quote is fabricated. Chuck Lorre didn't express regret over going \"too far\" with a subplot that never existed. In fact, Lorre’s vanity card at the end of that exact broadcast (Card #310) was an absurd fictional pitch for a show about an anger-management counselor with Tourette’s syndrome. When Lorre and the writers actually reflected on jokes they regretted from early seasons—most extensively in Jessica Radloff’s oral history of the show—their regrets centered on early misogynistic creepiness from Howard’s pickup tactics or dated transgender jokes, not an imaginary gay hookup in the telescope lab.\n\nThe physical geography of the episode makes an implied sexual encounter impossible anyway:\n1. **The Setting:** Howard, Raj, and Bernadette weren’t at Howard’s apartment drinking cocktails; they were trapped in the Caltech telescope control room overnight. \n2. **The Sequence:** Howard fell dead asleep on a chair. Raj, sober enough to be miserable and desperate, leaned in to kiss *Bernadette*. She pulled back in disgust, reprimanded him, and Raj spent the rest of the night groveling so she wouldn't tell Howard. \n3. **The Ending:** The episode didn’t cut to an awkward morning-after between two bros. It ended on the primary A-plot: Penny’s father Wyatt begging Leonard not to give up on Penny after catching on that the relationship charade was a lie.\n\nThe writers spent twelve seasons baiting the Howard-Raj dynamic—the mechanical lip-sync kissing machine in season five, joint couples therapy, Howard inspecting Raj’s chest for gynecomastia—but in \"The Boyfriend Complexity,\" the only betrayal was Raj attempting to poach his best friend's girlfriend."
  },
  {
    "role": "user",
    "content": "Jacob, you shouldn’t have called it sodomy. It carries too much stigma against the already marginalized communities. Do you really want to further divide them?"
  },
